      "title": "PHAR2001_1_4_5_Omeprazole",
      "library": "H5P.QuestionSet 1.21",
      "params": {
        "introPage": {
          "showIntroPage": false,
          "startButtonText": "Start Quiz",
          "introduction": ""
        },
        "progressType": "dots",
        "passPercentage": 50,
        "questions": [
          {
            "params": {
              "media": {
                "disableImageZooming": false
              },
              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Since proton pump inhibitors such as Omeprazole and other ...prazole drugs decrease stomach acid by nearly 100%, they are the first line therapy for people with Barrett's Disease and Peptic Ulcer Disease.&nbsp;"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Barrett's disease is a condition where there is oesophageal dysplasia, and it may progress to cancer if there is continued insult involving acid spashing up on the cells.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Peptic ulcer disease can result in a clinical emergency because continued acid can erode into a blood vessel, resulting in bleeding into the stomach.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>NO, no one should ever recommend that a client should go off their omeprazole unless they are a prescriber and unless they know the entire history of the client. The client could suffer from Barrett's disease or Peptic Ulcer disease, and both of those conditions need very long term treatment with a proton pump inhibitor.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "tip": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Indeed,&nbsp;no one should ever recommend that a client should go off their omeprazole unless they are a prescriber and unless they know the entire history of the client. The client could suffer from Barrett's disease or Peptic Ulcer disease, and both of those conditions need very long term treatment with a proton pump inhibitor.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                  },
                  "text": "<div>If a client has been on proton pump inhibitors like omeprazole for 5 or more years, the person should be withdrawn from the medication</div>\n"
                }
              ],
